# Every blob in this repository is LF. That was already the rule; until now it
# was enforced by a pre-commit whitespace check and by people remembering, which
# is why CRLF kept arriving on Windows: `core.autocrlf=true` writes CRLF into the
# working tree, and any tool that rewrites a file whole — a formatter, a script,
# an editor saving with platform endings — hands those CRLF bytes back to git as
# content. Without a rule here, git stores them.
#
# The default below normalises on the way in, so the working tree can be CRLF
# and the blob is still LF. Everything after it is an exception that the
# heuristic gets wrong in one direction or the other.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Binary: never touch these bytes.
#
# `text=auto` already leaves most of them alone — git looks for a NUL in the
# first 8k — but stating it is cheaper than trusting a heuristic with image
# data, and it also stops diffs from trying to render them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.png binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf binary
*.otf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.tgz binary
*.node binary
*.exe binary
*.dll binary
*.dylib binary
*.so binary
*.wasm binary
*.pdf binary

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Text the heuristic misreads as binary.
#
# These two suites embed a literal NUL to test that NUL is stripped from a
# download filename and from a partition key. `text=auto` finds that NUL and
# classifies the whole file as binary, which would exempt real TypeScript from
# the rule above — the exact hole this file exists to close. Both are LF today
# and must stay that way.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
src/mcp/__tests__/mcp-app-download.test.ts text eol=lf
src/shared/__tests__/mcp-app-partition.test.ts text eol=lf

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kept from before `* text=auto eol=lf` existed. Redundant with it now, and
# deliberately left in place: these are the files where a CRLF is not a
# cosmetic problem but a broken build, so they are worth stating twice.
# `sh` chokes on a CRLF shebang, and NSIS reads its own includes literally.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*.sh text eol=lf
*.command text eol=lf
build/**/*.txt text eol=lf
build/*.svg text eol=lf
resources/AGENTS.md* text eol=lf

# App-owned git hook shims must stay LF (sh chokes on CRLF shebangs)
scripts/hooks/pre-commit text eol=lf
scripts/hooks/pre-push text eol=lf
scripts/hooks/*.mjs text eol=lf
